From a3e004fd665e73f9b4179342ac25ffedfd8114b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Magnus Lindholm <linmag7@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:49:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] sparc32: do not gate HyperSPARC cache cross-calls on
 mm_cpumask

smp_flush_cache_mm(), smp_flush_cache_range() and smp_flush_cache_page()
cross-call the remote cache flush only when mm_cpumask names another CPU.
That mask tracks address-space use for TLB maintenance, not ownership of
cache lines. The task-switch path sets bits; it does not clear them on
switch-out. Older shootdown code narrowed the mask separately.

The HyperSPARC second-level cache is virtually indexed, physically tagged
and runs in copy-back mode. SPARC RISC User's Guide, ROSS Technology,
Third Edition, September 1993, section 4.4.2.4 defines Exclusive Modified
as "Only this cache module has a valid copy", that module being "the OWNER
of the cache line, and has the responsibility to update the next level of
memory"; Shared Modified likewise makes one module the owner. Section
4.4.3: "Flushing under copy-back cache mode for a modified cache line
means flushing the cache line into main memory and invalidating the cache
tag entry", and separately, "Unlike a TLB flush operation, all cache
flushing operations flush only one cache line at a time".

A dirty line therefore reaches memory only by eviction or by a flush on
the module that owns it, and none of that is tied to the TLB. A cpu that
stops running an mm keeps the lines it owns for that mm's pages; they
outlive the TLB entries and the mm_cpumask bit, and are written back when
evicted, by which time the page may have been freed and reused.

Flush on every cpu instead; smp_flush_cache_all() already cross-calls
unconditionally. Restrict this policy change to HyperSPARC; other cache
implementations retain their existing behavior.

Measured on a SPARCstation 20, two ROSS hyperSPARC modules at 150 MHz with
512 KB of cache each, mem=220M, on a fork-heavy shell workload. Fourteen
pairs of 45-second windows alternated the two behaviours within one boot:

  gated on mm_cpumask     70 faults in 6258 forks, 13 of 14 windows hit
  flushed on every cpu     0 faults in 5798 forks,  0 of 14 windows hit

Counting windows rather than faults, since faults arrive in bursts, that
is p = 3.7e-7. Fork throughput cost 7.1%, against 46% for flushing the
whole cache on every copy-on-write fault.

This needs the preceding index backends and supervisor-line coverage.
Request a dependent stable backport after those changes have merged.

Signed-off-by: Magnus Lindholm <linmag7@gmail.com>
---
 arch/sparc/mm/srmmu.c | 14 +++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/sparc/mm/srmmu.c b/arch/sparc/mm/srmmu.c
index 18d37c98a49a..0620a40f6f59 100644
--- a/arch/sparc/mm/srmmu.c
+++ b/arch/sparc/mm/srmmu.c
@@ -1686,10 +1686,18 @@ static bool any_other_mm_cpus(struct mm_struct *mm)
 	return cpumask_any_but(mm_cpumask(mm), smp_processor_id()) < nr_cpu_ids;
 }
 
+/* Dirty lines in a virtually indexed write-back cache outlive the mm_cpumask
+ * bit, so the mask cannot gate a cache flush the way it gates a shootdown.
+ */
+static bool cache_xcall_needed(struct mm_struct *mm)
+{
+	return is_hypersparc || any_other_mm_cpus(mm);
+}
+
 static void smp_flush_cache_mm(struct mm_struct *mm)
 {
 	if (mm->context != NO_CONTEXT) {
-		if (any_other_mm_cpus(mm))
+		if (cache_xcall_needed(mm))
 			xc1(local_ops->cache_mm, (unsigned long)mm);
 		local_ops->cache_mm(mm);
 	}
@@ -1711,7 +1719,7 @@ static void smp_flush_cache_range(struct vm_area_struct *vma,
 	struct mm_struct *mm = vma->vm_mm;
 
 	if (mm->context != NO_CONTEXT) {
-		if (any_other_mm_cpus(mm))
+		if (cache_xcall_needed(mm))
 			xc3(local_ops->cache_range, (unsigned long)vma, start,
 			    end);
 		local_ops->cache_range(vma, start, end);
@@ -1737,7 +1745,7 @@ static void smp_flush_cache_page(struct vm_area_struct *vma, unsigned long page)
 	struct mm_struct *mm = vma->vm_mm;
 
 	if (mm->context != NO_CONTEXT) {
-		if (any_other_mm_cpus(mm))
+		if (cache_xcall_needed(mm))
 			xc2(local_ops->cache_page, (unsigned long)vma, page);
 		local_ops->cache_page(vma, page);
 	}
-- 
